// @run
// @log hi!
// @log 4
// @log 6
// @hint Intentionally-unused function `_shout`
// @hint Intentionally-unused function `_idT`
// @hint Intentionally-unused function `_showPair`
// An UN-ANNOTATED parameter is an inference hole the CALL SITE fills: `monomorphize`
// (`compiler/emit_mono.vl`) flags such a decl generic (`monoIsGenericDecl`, `emit_base.vl`
// ~2775) and `monoInstantiate` REPLACES its `fnStmts` slot with a per-call-site instance.
// With no call site nothing replaces the slot, so the emitter lowers the TEMPLATE: the
// un-annotated param takes the default i32 valtype while the BODY's operator lowering picks
// its type from the other operand. `s + "!"` then emits the string-concat helper call over an
// i32 param — `vl check` rc 0, INVALID WASM.
//
// This file pins the three neighbours that DO work, so the hole is bounded from every side.
// The hole itself is not pinnable: it is check-clean invalid wasm, and `@emit-error` cannot
// pin that (`vl check --codegen` exits 0 and `vl build` writes the module before failing).
// Measured on `173367f`, each as a whole program with one live statement beside it:
//
//   function exclaim(s) { return s + "!" }     // never called -> INVALID WASM
//   function isA(s)     { return s == "a" }    // never called -> INVALID WASM
//   function outer() { function inner(s) { return s + "!" }  const _z = 1 }   // ditto
//   export function exclaim(s) { return s + "!" }   // ditto — and an exported entry point
//                                                   // has no in-module call site BY DESIGN
//
// `function add(a, b) { return a + b }` and `function half(x) { return x * 0.5 }` are NOT in
// the set: with both operands unpinned the lowering defaults to i32 too, so the two ends
// agree. The trigger is a body operand whose type the LITERAL forces (a string) while the
// param stays i32.
//
// THE PRUNE THAT ALREADY EXISTS AND THE MEMBER IT DECLINES. `monomorphize`'s tail loop
// (`emit_mono.vl` ~2286) replaces every still-un-instantiated decl with a no-op STUB — but
// only when `fnTyParams.length > 0`, i.e. an explicit `<T>`. Its header states the reason for
// the narrower gate: a lifted LAMBDA also has un-annotated params and is invoked through
// `call_ref`, never through `monoInstantiate`, so stubbing the broader `monoGen` set would
// silently no-op a live callback. That reason covers ANONYMOUS lambdas; it does not cover a
// NAMED decl, which is what the four rows above all are.
type Pair<A, B> = { a: A, b: B }

// (1) The SAME body, CALLED — the call site pins `s` to `string` and the instance lowers.
function exclaim(s) { return s + "!" }
print(exclaim("hi")) // hi!

// (2) The ANNOTATED twin, never called — annotated params need no instantiation, so the
//     template IS the final function and its param valtype is the annotation's.
function _shout(s: string) { return s + "!" }

// (3) The `<T>` twin, never called — the tail loop above stubs it out, which is exactly the
//     treatment row (1)'s uncalled form needs and does not get.
function _idT<T>(x: T): T { return x }

// A generic ALIAS application in an uncalled function's param is a SEPARATE gap with the
// opposite signature (a bystander RESCUES it, so it is a reservation miss, not this one):
// `collectA`'s `TypeRef` ladder (`emit_collect.vl` ~3050) is `<>`-blind, so `Pair<i32, string>`
// forces no `aUsed` and the string array heap type is never allocated. Here the program has
// other strings, so the type exists and the row runs — that IS the bystander control.
function _showPair(p: Pair<i32, string>) { return p.b }

function len2(a, b) { return a.length + b.length }
print(len2("ab", "cd")) // 4

function twice(n) { return n + n }
print(twice(3)) // 6
